<!-- ── 01 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-intro" data-code="intro">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Problem</div>
<h2>The same answer, computed a million times</h2>
<p>Serving a long context to a large language model happens in two phases. <strong>Prefill</strong> runs one forward pass over all <em>L</em> input tokens to populate the key–value (KV) cache; <strong>decode</strong> then generates output tokens one at a time, each attending to that cache. Prefill is the compute-heavy half, and its cost grows <em>super-linearly</em> in length.</p>
<p>Now look at how agents actually work. A popular knowledge-base article, a product manual, a regulatory filing — each is read by thousands of independent requests, sessions, and autonomous agents. Under today's serving model, every one of them re-prefills the <em>identical</em> text. N readers of one document perform N identical prefills. The same answer, computed a million times.</p>

<p>The paper's position is one sentence: <strong>when content is public and read by many, its KV cache is the natural unit of reuse.</strong> Compute it once, persist it as a portable artifact, and let every downstream agent pay a cheap load instead of a full prefill — like a content delivery network, except it caches <em>computation</em>, not bytes. They call it a <strong>prefill CDN</strong>.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">the_waste.viz — N agents, one document</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">toggle the two worlds</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="waste-canvas" class="viz-canvas" height="300"></canvas>
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <button class="canvas-btn active" id="waste-naive">Today: re-prefill</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="waste-cdn">Prefill CDN: reuse</button>
      <span class="ctrl-sep"></span>
      <button class="canvas-btn" id="waste-play">▶ Send agents</button>
    </div>
    <div class="readout">
      <div class="readout-item"><span class="readout-k">Agents served</span><span class="readout-v cyan" id="waste-served">0</span></div>
      <div class="readout-item"><span class="readout-k">Full prefills run</span><span class="readout-v" id="waste-prefills">0</span></div>
      <div class="readout-item"><span class="readout-k">Compute spent</span><span class="readout-v purple" id="waste-compute">0×</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<p class="canvas-label" style="margin-top:-.5rem">In <b>re-prefill</b>, every agent lights up red — a full prefill, identical to its neighbour's. In <b>reuse</b>, the publisher prefills <em>once</em> (red), then every agent just loads a green dot. After 50 agents that's 50 prefills vs. 1.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── 02 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-prefill" data-code="prefill">
<div class="section-no">02 — Background</div>
<h2>Prefill is the expensive part, and it gets worse with length</h2>
<p>A decoder-only transformer with <em>h</em> layers keeps, per layer, key and value tensors of shape <code>(batch, heads, seq, head_dim)</code>. Prefilling a context of length <em>L</em> fills these for all <em>L</em> positions. Two cost terms matter:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The dense projections do work proportional to <strong>L</strong> (linear).</li>
  <li>Self-attention does work proportional to <strong>L²</strong> (quadratic) — every token attends to every earlier one.</li>
</ul>
<p>A single continuation step over an <em>already-resident</em> cache, by contrast, grows far more slowly. So as documents get longer, the ratio between "prefill it fresh" and "reuse the cache" doesn't just stay favourable — it <em>widens</em>. The chart below is the paper's Table 1, measured on Qwen3-4B (fp16, Apple M1 Pro / MPS).</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">cost_vs_length.viz — prefill vs. one reuse step</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover the points</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="length-canvas" class="viz-canvas" height="340"></canvas>
    <p class="canvas-label" style="margin-top:.875rem">Prefill time climbs steeply (the L² term); the reuse step stays nearly flat. The speedup printed on each prefill point grows from <span class="canvas-val">8.6×</span> at 255 tokens to <span class="canvas-val">49.7×</span> at 3774 — the longer the document, the bigger the win.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout cyan">
<b>A concrete artifact.</b> For Qwen3-4B (36 layers, 8 KV heads × head-dim 128, grouped-query attention), the KV cache grows at ≈ <b>0.148 MB/token</b>. A ~3.8K-token document yields a <b>557 MB</b> artifact. That linear size against prefill's super-linear compute is exactly why long documents are both the most expensive to prefill and the most rewarding to cache.</div>
</section>

<!-- ── 03 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-exact" data-code="exact">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Key Idea</div>
<h2>Reuse is token-exact — not "close enough"</h2>
<p>The reason this works at all: under a fixed model with deterministic kernels, the KV cache is a <strong>deterministic function of the input</strong>. Because attention is causal, the cache entry for position <em>i</em> depends only on tokens 1…<em>i</em> — never on anything that comes later. So the entries for a document's tokens are <em>bit-for-bit identical</em> whether you prefilled the document alone or prefilled the document followed by a question.</p>
<p>That means loading a saved cache and continuing produces <strong>the same tokens</strong> as prefilling the whole thing from scratch. The paper verifies it: under greedy decoding, <strong>24/24 tokens match</strong>, with a maximum absolute logit difference of just <strong>0.02</strong> — pure floating-point noise, not a semantic difference.</p>

<p>Two details make it exact. Get either wrong and you reproduce the "shifted" failure mode where outputs diverge:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">generate_from_kv.py — the load-and-continue recipe</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">step through it</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="step-list" id="exact-steps">
      <div class="step-box" data-step="0">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Publisher: SaveKV (one-time)</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Run one forward pass over the document with <code>use_cache=True</code>, then serialize the per-layer K/V tensors plus metadata <code>(model, dtype, L)</code>.</div>
          <div class="step-code">a = serialize(forward(c).cache, m=(model, dtype, L))</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="1">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Agent: deserialize &amp; check metadata</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Load the artifact and <em>reject mismatches</em> — a cache is only valid for the exact (model, dtype) pair that produced it.</div>
          <div class="step-code">K, m = deserialize(a); assert m.model == self.model</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="2">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Detail 1 — position ids start at L</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Continuation tokens get position ids L, L+1, … so RoPE matches exactly what a from-scratch pass would use.</div>
          <div class="step-code">pos = [start, …, start + len(cur) − 1]   # start = m.L</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="3">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Detail 2 — full attention mask</div>
          <div class="step-desc">The mask must span all L + |q| positions so the continuation attends to the <em>entire</em> cached context, not just itself.</div>
          <div class="step-code">mask = ones(L + len(cur))</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-box" data-step="4">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="step-title">Decode — token-exact</div>
          <div class="step-desc">Forward over the continuation, argmax, append. Result: 24/24 greedy tokens match the from-scratch path; max logit diff 0.02.</div>
          <div class="step-code">y_t = argmax(forward(cur, K, pos, mask).logits[−1])</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="step-nav">
      <button id="exact-prev">‹ Prev</button>
      <button id="exact-next">Next ›</button>
      <span class="step-prog" id="exact-prog">Step 1 of 5</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="callout">
<b>Why only prefixes?</b> The exactness argument relies on the cached positions being a causal <em>prefix</em>. If you stitch together caches of two fragments prefilled <em>independently</em>, the second never attended to the first — cross-attention is missing, and exactness breaks. That's the regime fusion methods like CacheBlend repair with selective recomputation. This paper deliberately studies the simplest, free case: one document as a shared prefix.</div>
</section>

<!-- ── 04 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-cost" data-code="cost">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Cost Model</div>
<h2>The break-even is immediate — reuse pays off on the second read</h2>
<p>Here's the whole economic argument in two lines. For one document, let <em>C<sub>prefill</sub></em> be the cost to compute its KV and <em>C<sub>load</sub></em> the cost to load and reuse it. Over <em>N</em> reuses:</p>

<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">Per-call cost over N reuses</div>
from-scratch:&nbsp;&nbsp;per-call = <span class="math-var">C_prefill</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment"># flat — every reader pays full price</span><br>
KV-reuse:&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;per-call = <span class="math-var">C_prefill</span> / <span class="math-var">N</span> + <span class="math-var">C_load</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment"># falls toward a floor of C_load</span><br>
break-even:&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;N* = <span class="math-var">C_prefill</span> / (<span class="math-var">C_prefill</span> − <span class="math-var">C_load</span>)&nbsp;&nbsp;→&nbsp;1&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="math-comment"># when C_load ≪ C_prefill</span>
</div>

<p>Because the load is so much cheaper than the prefill, the break-even count <em>N*</em> lands in the range <strong>[1.02, 1.13]</strong> — reuse wins by the <em>second</em> read. Drag the slider below to watch the amortized per-call cost collapse toward the floor, and switch document length to see how the absolute saving balloons.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">break_even.viz — amortized per-call cost vs. reuse count N</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">drag N · pick a length</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="cost-canvas" class="viz-canvas" height="360"></canvas>
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <span class="canvas-label">Document:</span>
      <button class="canvas-btn" data-len="255">255 tok</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" data-len="945">945 tok</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn" data-len="1888">1888 tok</button>
      <button class="canvas-btn active" data-len="3774">3774 tok</button>
    </div>
    <div class="canvas-controls">
      <span class="canvas-label">Reuses N = <span class="canvas-val" id="cost-n-val">100</span></span>
      <input type="range" class="slider" id="cost-n" min="0" max="100" value="40">
    </div>
    <div class="readout">
      <div class="readout-item"><span class="readout-k">Per-call (re-prefill)</span><span class="readout-v" id="cost-flat">14.71 s</span></div>
      <div class="readout-item"><span class="readout-k">Per-call (KV reuse)</span><span class="readout-v green" id="cost-amort">0.45 s</span></div>
      <div class="readout-item"><span class="readout-k">Cheaper by</span><span class="readout-v purple" id="cost-ratio">32×</span></div>
      <div class="readout-item"><span class="readout-k">Total compute saved</span><span class="readout-v cyan" id="cost-saved">—</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<p class="canvas-label" style="margin-top:-.5rem">The grey line is flat — re-prefill costs the same no matter how many readers. The green curve is <em>C<sub>prefill</sub>/N + C<sub>load</sub></em>: it crashes after the first reuse and flattens at the load floor. At N=1000, the 3774-token document saves ~1.4 × 10⁴ seconds of compute.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── 05 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-host" data-code="host">
<div class="section-no">05 — The Twist</div>
<h2>Where the KV lives decides whether the market exists</h2>
<p>The cost model above ignores one thing a real service must pay: storing and <em>moving</em> the artifact. This is where the naive idea — "ship the KV to whoever buys it" — falls apart, and where the paper's actual proposal comes in.</p>
<p>The KV cache is high-entropy and nearly incompressible losslessly. So if you ship it, you egress the whole multi-hundred-megabyte artifact <em>per load</em>. The arithmetic is brutal:</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">ship_vs_host.viz — two delivery models</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">the network is the enemy</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="host-canvas" class="viz-canvas" height="320"></canvas>
    <p class="canvas-label" style="margin-top:.875rem"><b style="color:#f87171">Ship</b>: the 0.54 GB artifact crosses the network every load — at $0.09/GB egress that's <b>$0.049</b>, which is <b>2.6×</b> the <b>$0.019</b> of prefill it saves. Shipping <em>loses money</em>. <b style="color:var(--green)">Host</b>: the cache never leaves the provider's datacenter; the agent sends a tiny query and is billed a cache-read. No egress at all.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>A storage-aware cost model</h3>
<p>Adding storage price <em>σ</em> (per byte per unit time, over horizon <em>T</em>) and transfer cost <em>β</em> (per byte, once per load) for an artifact of size <em>s</em>:</p>
<div class="math-block">
<div class="math-label">Storage-aware total over N loads</div>
total_KV(<span class="math-var">N</span>) = <span class="math-var">C_prefill</span> + <span class="math-var">σ·s·T</span> + <span class="math-var">N</span>·(<span class="math-var">C_load</span> + <span class="math-var">β·s</span>)<br>
<span class="math-comment"># effective per-load floor rises from C_load to  C_load + β·s + σ·s·T/N</span><br>
<span class="math-comment"># shipping: β·s caps how cheap a load can get → compression matters</span><br>
<span class="math-comment"># hosting: β = 0 (no egress); σ·s·T/N vanishes as N grows</span>
</div>
<p>The transfer term <em>β·s</em> caps how cheap a shipped load can get — which is exactly why lossless KV compression matters for the <em>economics</em>, not just storage. Hosting sets <em>β = 0</em> and the storage term amortizes away with reuse. Production prompt-caching APIs already work this way: the cache stays in the datacenter, and you're billed a per-load cache-read rate.</p>
</section>

<!-- ── 06 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-market" data-code="market">
<div class="section-no">06 — The Prize</div>
<h2>Millions of dollars of margin per popular document</h2>
<p>Put it together at scale. Take the paper's largest measured point — one hot 3774-token document — and serve it to <strong>80 million agents</strong>. The economic ceiling is set by the <em>measured</em> compute saving, not by any quoted price ratio. Re-prefilling for everyone costs ~$1.5M; serving from a hosted cache at the true marginal compute cost is ~$0.03M.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">market.viz — cost to serve 80M agents one document</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">lower is better</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="results-bars" id="market-bars">
      <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label">re-prefill (today)</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="100" style="background:rgba(107,114,128,.55)"><span>$1.51M · 1.0×</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label">ship raw fp16 (egress)</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="100" style="background:#ef4444"><span>$3.9M · 0.4× — loses</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label">ship int8 (lossy)</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="53" style="background:var(--amber)"><span>$2.0M · 0.8× — loses, inexact</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label">host, priced (0.1× tariff)</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="10" style="background:var(--purple)"><span>$0.15M · 10×</span></div></div></div>
      <div class="bar-row"><div class="bar-label">host, physical (measured)</div>
        <div class="bar-track"><div class="bar-fill" data-w="2" style="background:var(--green)"><span>$0.03M · 49.7×</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The two hosting rows are the punchline. APIs charge a <strong>0.1× cache-read tariff</strong> — a 10× user-facing discount. But the paper's measurements show the underlying compute is <strong>49.7× cheaper</strong>. The gap between the priced 10× and the physical ~50× is <strong>provider margin</strong>: millions of dollars per popular document. And it answers the obvious objection — "isn't the 10× discount arbitrary?" No: the 10× is a <em>floor</em> grounded in physics, which the measured saving comfortably clears.</p>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Serve N=80M</th><th>vs. re-prefill</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>re-prefill (today)</td><td class="mn">$1,509,600</td><td class="mn">1.0×</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ship raw fp16 (lossless, egress)</td><td class="mn">$3.9M</td><td class="mn bad">0.4× loses</td></tr>
    <tr><td>ship int8 (2×, lossy)</td><td class="mn">$2.0M</td><td class="mn bad">0.8× loses, inexact</td></tr>
    <tr><td>host, priced (0.1× tariff)</td><td class="mn">$150,960</td><td class="mn">10.0×</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td>host, physical (measured)</td><td class="mn">$30,370</td><td class="mn best">49.7×</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout green">
<b>What's built, what's open.</b> The substrate is mature: prior work makes KV reuse <em>possible</em> (fusion), <em>storable</em> (flash/CSD), <em>movable</em> (disaggregated serving), and <em>small</em> (compression). What's missing — and what this paper frames — is the cross-party economic layer: pricing, cache invalidation on model upgrade, provenance, and a fallback-to-local-prefill on miss. There's also the open technical problem of <em>lossless</em> KV compression, since the int8 probe already broke exactness (16/32 tokens).</div>

<h3>The honest caveats</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Model-bound.</strong> An artifact is meaningless across models or precisions — this only works inside a standardized-model ecosystem.</li>
  <li><strong>Single device, single model.</strong> All numbers are Qwen3-4B / fp16 / Apple M1 Pro. Absolute speedups are device-specific; the qualitative trend (reuse ≪ prefill, gap widening with L) follows from the L² term and should hold broadly, but server-GPU and cross-model confirmation is future work.</li>
  <li><strong>Memory-stable range only.</strong> They deliberately exclude the ≥ 7.5K-token regime, where prefill on this device inflates super-linearly under memory pressure and would <em>overstate</em> the ratio. The reported 9–50× is the honest range.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<!-- Listen-friendly content (hidden summary played during audio playback) -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display: none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: Can I Buy Your KV Cache?</h2>
    <p>Large language models serve long contexts in two phases. Prefill runs a forward pass over every input token to build the key-value cache, and its cost grows super-linearly with length because self-attention scales with the square of the sequence length. Decode then generates tokens one at a time. Prefill is the expensive half. The problem this paper highlights is that across the world, AI agents reading the same popular document each re-run prefill from scratch, rebuilding an identical KV cache. N readers of one document perform N identical, redundant prefills.</p>
    <p>The proposal is almost offensively simple: compute the KV cache once, let a publisher host it, and let every other agent buy the right to load it and skip prefill. This is token-exact, because the KV cache is a deterministic, causal function of the input — a document's cache entries are bit-for-bit identical whether prefilled alone or as part of a longer sequence. Under greedy decoding the authors observe a perfect twenty-four out of twenty-four token match, with a maximum logit difference of just point-zero-two. Two details make it exact: continuation tokens must get position ids starting at L so rotary embeddings line up, and the attention mask must span the full context.</p>
    <p>The cost model is the heart of it. Re-prefilling costs the same flat amount per reader. KV reuse costs C-prefill divided by N, plus the cheap load cost, which collapses toward a floor after the very first reuse. The break-even reuse count is essentially one — reuse pays off by the second read. On Qwen3-4B, reuse is between nine and fifty times cheaper than prefill, and the gap widens with document length.</p>
    <p>The crucial design choice is where the cache lives. Shipping the artifact fails: the KV is nearly incompressible, so per-load network egress costs more than the prefill it saves. Hosting it provider-side, exactly like production prompt caching, removes egress entirely. Serving one hot document to eighty million agents costs about one and a half million dollars to re-prefill, but only about thirty thousand dollars of reuse compute — nearly fifty times less. The ten-times cache-read discount APIs charge is a physically grounded floor, and the gap to the measured fifty-times saving is provider margin: millions of dollars per popular document.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：我可以买你的 KV 缓存吗？</h2>
    <p>大语言模型处理长上下文分为两个阶段。预填充对所有输入 token 做一次前向传播来构建键值缓存，由于自注意力的开销与序列长度的平方成正比，预填充的成本随长度超线性增长。随后的解码阶段才逐个生成 token。预填充是昂贵的那一半。这篇论文指出的问题是：全世界的 AI 智能体在读取同一份热门文档时,都各自从零重新运行预填充,重建出一份完全相同的 KV 缓存。一份文档被 N 个读者读取,就要执行 N 次完全相同的冗余预填充。</p>
    <p>论文的提议简单到近乎离谱:只计算一次 KV 缓存,让发布者托管它,让其他每个智能体购买加载它的权利,从而跳过预填充。这是逐 token 精确的,因为 KV 缓存是输入的确定性因果函数——一份文档的缓存条目,无论是单独预填充还是作为更长序列的一部分预填充,都是逐比特相同的。在贪心解码下,作者观察到二十四分之二十四的完美 token 匹配,最大 logit 差异仅为零点零二。两个细节保证精确性:续写 token 的位置 id 必须从 L 开始,以对齐旋转位置编码;注意力掩码必须覆盖完整上下文。</p>
    <p>成本模型是核心。重新预填充对每个读者的成本是固定不变的。而 KV 复用的成本是 C-prefill 除以 N 再加上廉价的加载成本,这个值在第一次复用之后就迅速逼近下限。盈亏平衡的复用次数基本上等于一——复用在第二次读取时就已经划算。在 Qwen3-4B 上,复用比预填充便宜九到五十倍,而且文档越长,差距越大。</p>
    <p>关键的设计选择是缓存存放在哪里。把缓存发送给购买方是行不通的:KV 几乎不可压缩,所以每次加载的网络出口流量成本比它节省的预填充还要高。把它托管在服务商一侧,就像生产环境的提示缓存一样,完全消除了出口流量。把一份热门文档服务给八千万个智能体,重新预填充要花约一百五十万美元,而复用计算只需约三万美元——便宜近五十倍。API 收取的十倍缓存读取折扣是一个有物理依据的下限,而它与实测五十倍节省之间的差距,就是服务商的利润:每份热门文档可达数百万美元。</p>
  </div>
</div>

</article>

